---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import { getCaseStudy } from '../../data/caseStudies';
import HoverVideo from '../../components/islands/HoverVideo';

const study = getCaseStudy('sensecast');
---
<ArticleLayout study={study}>

    <section id="problem" class="cs-section">
      <h2 class="reveal">Problem</h2>
      <p class="lede">Public environments are unpredictable — noise, light and crowd levels change constantly. For people with sensory sensitivities or anxiety, that unpredictability can be overwhelming and lead to avoidance.</p>
      <p>Existing solutions, such as occupancy apps and weather forecasts, address the practical layer but fail to address the emotional layer of spatial experience.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="process" class="cs-section">
      <h2 class="reveal">Process</h2>
      <div class="cs-pull">
        <p>"How might we visualise sensory conditions in a way that reduces emotional discomfort and builds trust before people enter a space?"</p>
        <cite>Problem statement</cite>
      </div>
      <ol class="cs-numbered">
        <li><strong>Research &amp; discovery.</strong> <span>Investigated how unpredictable public spaces affect people with sensory sensitivities, through surveys, interviews and psychological studies.</span></li>
        <li><strong>Concept development.</strong> <span>Developed personas and user journeys, and framed the core challenge: translating ambient data into emotional previews.</span></li>
        <li><strong>Ideation &amp; visual language.</strong> <span>Explored how shape, colour and motion influence emotion, and defined a calming, human-centred visual style.</span></li>
        <li><strong>Design &amp; prototyping.</strong> <span>Created wireframes and interactive prototypes for mobile, public screens and wearables, focused on intuitive user flows.</span></li>
        <li><strong>Technical exploration.</strong> <span>Built audio-reactive visualisations in TouchDesigner and used AI tools to accelerate iteration and mock-up creation.</span></li>
        <li><strong>Testing &amp; delivery.</strong> <span>Delivered a cohesive brand identity, functional prototypes, and a real-time visualisation concept demonstrating the full user experience.</span></li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section id="research-grounding-in-human-needs" class="cs-section">
      <h2 class="reveal">Research — grounding in human needs</h2>
      <p>Surveys and interviews explored how people perceive and navigate public spaces. Psychological studies investigated how shapes, colours and sensory inputs affect emotional response. Benchmarking against weather apps and ambient interfaces showed how soft, uncertain data can still be presented clearly.</p>

      <div class="cs-figure-pair reveal">
        <figure class="cs-figure">
          <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig1-survey-1.png" alt="Survey pie chart: 'What emotions do you associate with a GP experience?' — 16 responses, split mainly between anxious (31.3%), comfortable (31.3%), relaxed (12.5%) and distracted (12.5%)." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
          <figcaption>Fig. 1 — survey: emotional associations with a GP waiting room.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="cs-figure">
          <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig2-survey-2.png" alt="Survey pie chart: 'What emotions do you associate with a university cafeteria?' — 16 responses, dominated by overstimulated (37.5%), with relaxed and comfortable each at 18.8%." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
          <figcaption>Fig. 2 — survey: emotional associations with a university cafeteria.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-figure-pair reveal">
        <figure class="cs-figure">
          <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig3-sensory-installations-1.png" alt="Research board on sensory design installations, referencing existing immersive and calming spatial experiences." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
          <figcaption>Fig. 3 — research into sensory design installations.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="cs-figure">
          <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig4-sensory-installations-2.png" alt="A second research board on sensory design installations, continuing the survey of existing calming spatial experiences." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
          <figcaption>Fig. 4 — research into sensory design installations, continued.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-figure-pair reveal">
        <figure class="cs-figure">
          <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig5-visual-language-research-1.png" alt="Research board on sensory visual language, collecting reference imagery for how soft data and forecasts are visualised elsewhere." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
          <figcaption>Fig. 5 — research into sensory visual language and forecasting interfaces.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="cs-figure">
          <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig6-visual-language-research-2.png" alt="A second research board on forecasting user interfaces, continuing the survey of visual language reference imagery." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
          <figcaption>Fig. 6 — research into forecasting user interfaces, continued.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <div class="cs-quote">
        <p>A calm and emotionally supportive interface emerges from the thoughtful combination of sensory science and visual design. Rounded shapes, organic forms and soft colour transitions turn complex sensory information into an accessible, reassuring experience — a principle that became central to SenseCast's visual language.</p>
        <cite>Key insight</cite>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="concept-development-from-idea-to-vision" class="cs-section">
      <h2 class="reveal">Concept development — from idea to vision</h2>
      <p>Role-based personas and user journeys mapped needs and behaviours. Key sensory inputs — sound, light, crowd density, colour and social cues — were identified, and an impact–feasibility matrix prioritised the most meaningful data. The concept settled on transforming ambient data into emotional previews that support comfort and predictability.</p>
      <p class="lede">Design concept: a predictive "emotional weather forecast" for spaces, visualised through soft, fluid graphics that represent sensory conditions at a glance.</p>

      <div class="cs-figure-pair reveal">
        <figure class="cs-figure">
          <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig7-personas-1.png" alt="Role-based persona board, mapping one target user's goals, environment and sensory triggers." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
          <figcaption>Fig. 7 — role-based persona.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="cs-figure">
          <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig8-personas-2.png" alt="A second role-based persona board, mapping a different target user's goals, environment and sensory triggers." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
          <figcaption>Fig. 8 — a second role-based persona.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <figure class="cs-figure reveal">
        <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig9-journey-map.png" alt="User journey map tracing a person's emotional state before, during and after entering an unfamiliar public space." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
        <figcaption>Fig. 9 — user journey mapping.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="cs-figure reveal">
        <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig10-feasibility-matrix.png" alt="Impact-feasibility matrix plotting candidate sensory data sources against how emotionally meaningful and how technically feasible each was." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
        <figcaption>Fig. 10 — feasibility–impact diagram.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="cs-figure reveal">
        <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig11-sensory-inputs-map.png" alt="Diagram mapping key sensory inputs — sound, light, spatial layout, smell, colour and social cues — by their emotional impact and technical feasibility." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
        <figcaption>Fig. 11 — mapping key sensory inputs by emotional impact and technical feasibility.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="cs-figure reveal">
        <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig12-sensory-zones.png" alt="Diagram defining sensory zones based on measurable variables like sound levels, light intensity and crowd density, categorising environments from a 'safe bubble' to 'unpredictable'." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
        <figcaption>Fig. 12 — defining sensory zones, from "safe bubble" to "unpredictable".</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section id="design-prototyping-bringing-sensecast-to-life" class="cs-section">
      <h2 class="reveal">Design &amp; prototyping — bringing SenseCast to life</h2>
      <h3>Sensory visual explorations</h3>
      <p>TouchDesigner translated sensory data into dynamic visuals — audio-reactive elements, fluid forms and particle systems standing in for sound, light and crowd density. The learning curve with the tool expanded technical range and deepened an understanding of how data can drive emotional, atmospheric design.</p>
      <figure class="cs-figure reveal">
        <div class="cs-figure-stack">
          <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig13-audioreactive-exploration-1.png" alt="Audio-reactive visual exploration in TouchDesigner: a glowing fluid texture reacting to sound." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
          <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig14-audioreactive-exploration-2.png" alt="A second audio-reactive visual exploration, a different fluid particle texture reacting to sound." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
          <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig15-audioreactive-exploration-3.png" alt="A third audio-reactive visual exploration, continuing the study of fluid, particle-based sound-reactive textures." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
        </div>
        <figcaption>Fig. 13–15 — audio-reactive visual exploration in TouchDesigner.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="cs-figure reveal">
        <HoverVideo client:visible src="/public/images/sensecast/fig16-audioreactive-video.mov" preload="metadata" />
        <figcaption>Fig. 16 — an audio-reactive visual, in motion.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h3>Brand identity design</h3>
      <p>The logo and visual language use fluid forms and glassmorphism effects to capture the idea of "casting" sensory data, while maintaining a calm and approachable character.</p>
      <figure class="cs-figure reveal">
        <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig17-brand-identity.png" alt="SenseCast brand identity board: logo lockup variations in six colourways, an Inter/Bebas Neue typography pairing, a six-colour palette, and organic pattern swatches generated from real-time data." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
        <figcaption>Fig. 17 — brand identity of SenseCast.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>Wireframing and structure: wireframes and user flows defined the core interactions and navigation of the product.</p>
      <figure class="cs-figure reveal">
        <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig18-wireframing.png" alt="Low-fidelity wireframe flow for the SenseCast app, defining core screens and navigation." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
        <figcaption>Fig. 18 — wireframing.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h3>Vibecoding &amp; creative process</h3>
      <p>Vibecoding — working conversationally with an AI coding assistant — sped up iteration and acted as a creative partner, generating new visual directions and strengthening the ability to combine computational logic with emotional storytelling.</p>
      <figure class="cs-figure reveal">
        <div class="cs-figure-stack">
          <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig19-vibecoded-1.png" alt="Vibecoded UI outcome: an AI-assisted coding session generating part of the SenseCast interface." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
          <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig20-vibecoded-2.png" alt="A second vibecoded UI outcome, continuing the AI-assisted interface exploration." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
          <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig21-vibecoded-3.png" alt="A third vibecoded UI outcome, continuing the AI-assisted interface exploration." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
          <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig22-vibecoded-4.png" alt="A fourth vibecoded UI outcome, continuing the AI-assisted interface exploration." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
          <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig23-vibecoded-5.png" alt="A fifth vibecoded UI outcome, concluding the AI-assisted interface exploration." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
        </div>
        <figcaption>Fig. 19–23 — vibecoded outcomes.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h3>UI design</h3>
      <p>High-fidelity interfaces for mobile, public screens and a wearable extension used soft gradients, organic shapes and a calming colour palette, translating complex sensory data into clear, emotionally supportive experiences.</p>
      <div class="cs-figure-pair reveal">
        <figure class="cs-figure">
          <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig24-hifi-mobile-1.png" alt="High-fidelity mobile screen showing a destination's live conditions: an optimal status prediction, sound level, crowd density, lighting, air quality readings, and environmental temperature and humidity." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
          <figcaption>Fig. 24 — high-fidelity mobile prototype.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="cs-figure">
          <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig25-hifi-mobile-2.png" alt="A second high-fidelity mobile screen, continuing the live sensory-conditions flow for a destination." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
          <figcaption>Fig. 25 — high-fidelity mobile prototype, continued.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <figure class="cs-figure reveal">
        <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig26-hifi-public-screen.png" alt="High-fidelity public-screen prototype, showing an ambient forecast visualisation designed for a shared space rather than a personal device." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
        <figcaption>Fig. 26 — high-fidelity public-screen prototype.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section id="testing-future-steps" class="cs-section">
      <h2 class="reveal">Testing &amp; future steps</h2>
      <p>The concept was tested beyond the screen too: a public-screen projection let people experience the ambient forecast in a shared space, and peer review sessions gathered reactions to colour and texture treatments across a set of simulated destinations, testing which visual moods read as calm, safe or overwhelming, and to whom.</p>
      <figure class="cs-figure reveal">
        <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig27-video-showcase.jpeg" alt="Photo of the SenseCast concept projected onto a public screen in a workshop space, alongside a smaller vertical display showing the same ambient forecast." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
        <figcaption>Fig. 27 — video showcase and public-screen projection.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="cs-figure reveal">
        <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig28-peer-review.png" alt="Six phone-lock-screen mockups in different colour treatments — orange, blue, red, purple, lavender and teal — each annotated with sticky-note reviewer comments describing how crowded, calm or overwhelming that colour and texture combination felt." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
        <figcaption>Fig. 28 — peer review on UI and colour iterations.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="cs-figure reveal">
        <img src="/public/images/sensecast/fig29-audioreactive-final.png" alt="A final audio-reactive visual study, refining the fluid, particle-based texture language used across the brand and interface." loading="lazy" onerror="imgFallback(this)">
        <figcaption>Fig. 29 — an audio-reactive visual, refined.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section id="next-time-i-would" class="cs-section">
      <h2 class="reveal">Next time, I would…</h2>
      <ol class="cs-numbered">
        <li><strong>Place more emphasis on testing.</strong> <span>Deeper, more frequent user testing throughout the process would have given stronger insight into how people emotionally respond to the visuals and interactions.</span></li>
        <li><strong>Address accessibility in the visual design.</strong> <span>The glassmorphism style achieved a calm, soothing aesthetic, but its accessibility limitations needed more thorough attention — particularly given a target audience that is sensitive and requires clear, inclusive design.</span></li>
        <li><strong>Improve the showcase experience.</strong> <span>The projection concept communicated the idea well, but the presentation and setup could have been refined further for a more impactful showcase.</span></li>
      </ol>
    </section>
</ArticleLayout>
